-- Migration 34: let revlog.reviewed_at carry a supplied value.
--
-- Migration 32 declared:
--
--   DEFINE FIELD reviewed_at ON revlog DEFAULT time::now()
--       VALUE $before OR time::now();
--
-- A field's VALUE clause is evaluated after the statement's input and
-- REPLACES it. So `CREATE revlog SET ... reviewed_at = <anything>` does not
-- write that value - it writes the current time. Measured:
--
--   CREATE revlog SET reviewed_at = '2020-01-01T00:00:00+00:00'
--   -> stored reviewed_at = 2026-10-13T11:43:23  (the insert time)
--
-- The app's own writes were never affected: record_grade() already passes
-- time::now() explicitly, which is the same thing. It only breaks when
-- something needs to import a review that happened *earlier* - which is
-- precisely what sync does.
--
-- Two consequences, both bad:
--   1. Review history is falsified on the receiving device. Every imported
--      review is timestamped at import, so "what did I study last week"
--      becomes "what did I import last week", and any future scheduling
--      replay from the log reads the wrong intervals.
--   2. Deduplication can never match, because every import has a unique
--      timestamp. The revlog grows without bound on every sync.
--
-- DROP VALUE and keep DEFAULT: a supplied value now wins, and an absent
-- one still falls back to time::now(). Revlog rows are append-only and
-- never updated, so $before had nothing to preserve here.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE reviewed_at ON TABLE revlog TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();